---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Grade from '../components/Grade.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
---
<Base
  terms
  title="Campus safety and sexual misconduct: the public record"
  description="A neutral, sourced summary of Harvard University’s public record on sexual assault and harassment: federal findings, climate surveys, Clery statistics and the documented outcomes of faculty cases."
  eyebrow="Campus safety"
  lede="This page summarises what official records and established reporting show about sexual misconduct at Harvard and how the University has responded. It is a reference for people weighing institutional culture. It is separate from the donor material elsewhere on this site."
  reviewed="2026-11-20"
  basis="Federal civil-rights records; Harvard’s own surveys and reports"
>
  <Callout title="How this page treats individuals" plain>
    <p>
      Only institutional findings, announced sanctions and court outcomes are reported, each attributed to its source.
      An allegation is labelled as an allegation. Where a person has denied the claims, that is stated. Nothing here
      is a finding by this site.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="where">Where to find current numbers</h2>
  <p>Figures change every year. These are the primary sources to read directly.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Annual Security Report.</strong> Federal law (the Clery Act) requires Harvard’s police department to publish campus crime statistics each October, including reported rape and fondling.<Cite id="hupd-asr" /> Counts depend on which locations are included, so read the tables and their notes together.</li>
    <li><strong>Student surveys.</strong> Harvard took part in national surveys in 2015 and 2019 and ran a comparable survey in 2024.<Cite id="harvard-2024-hesma" /></li>
    <li><strong>Complaint data.</strong> Harvard publishes annual figures on disclosures and formal complaints. The office responsible has been reorganised more than once, so check the University’s site for the current publisher.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="surveys">What students reported</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap full">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption>Headline results as reported. Measures differ between years; use Harvard’s own tables for exact comparison.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Survey</th><th>Participation</th><th>Headline result</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>2015 (Association of American Universities)</th><td data-label="Participation">Above 50 percent</td><td data-label="Headline result">About 31 percent of senior undergraduate women reported some form of nonconsensual sexual contact during their time at Harvard; 16 percent reported completed or attempted penetration.</td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A2" /><Cite id="hm-2015-aau" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>2019 (Association of American Universities)</th><td data-label="Participation">About 36 percent</td><td data-label="Headline result">About a third of undergraduate women surveyed reported some form of nonconsensual sexual contact.</td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A2" /><Cite id="crimson-2019-aau" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>2024 (Harvard survey)</th><td data-label="Participation">Above 35 percent</td><td data-label="Headline result">Harvard reported a statistically significant decline since 2019 and described prevalence as still “alarmingly high.”</td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A1" /><Cite id="harvard-2024-hesma" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The 2015 figure describes seniors and the 2019 figure describes all undergraduate women surveyed, so the two are
    not a like-for-like trend.
  </p>

  <h2 id="federal">Federal civil-rights findings</h2>
  <h3>Harvard Law School, 2014</h3>
  <p>
    On 30 December 2014 the U.S. Department of Education’s Office for Civil Rights announced that Harvard Law School
    had violated Title IX in how it responded to complaints of sexual harassment and assault. The findings included
    that its procedures did not meet the requirement of a prompt and equitable response and that it had used a standard
    of proof higher than the Department then required.<Cite id="ocr-2014-letter" /> The school signed a resolution
    agreement committing it to revise its policies, train staff and review earlier complaint files.<Cite id="ocr-2014-agreement" /><Cite id="crimson-2014-ocr" />
  </p>
  <h3>Harvard College, opened 2014</h3>
  <p>
    A separate federal investigation into Harvard College’s handling of sexual assault complaints opened in April 2014
    and was still reported as open in 2018.<Cite id="crimson-2018-college-ocr" /> We have not located a public record
    of how it was resolved.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cases">Faculty cases with documented institutional outcomes</h2>
  <p>Three cases produced public findings or sanctions by Harvard. They are summarised from the University’s announcements and contemporaneous reporting.</p>

  <h3>Jorge Domínguez (Government)</h3>
  <p>
    In February 2018 the Chronicle of Higher Education reported allegations of sexual harassment against Professor
    Domínguez spanning decades, and that Harvard had disciplined him for misconduct in 1983.<Cite id="chronicle-2018-dominguez" />
    He was placed on leave and retired. In May 2019 the Faculty of Arts and Sciences announced that a Title IX
    investigation had found unwelcome sexual conduct toward several individuals over nearly four decades. It revoked his
    emeritus status and barred him from the campus.<Cite id="hm-2019-dominguez" /> His own public responses are recorded
    in the cited reporting.
  </p>
  <p>
    Harvard then commissioned an External Review Committee. Its 2021 report concluded that pronounced power
    disparities, inadequate reporting mechanisms and a permissive culture had allowed the conduct to continue, and that
    keeping the 1983 sanction confidential may have deterred later reports. It made recommendations including central
    vetting of personnel records before appointments.<Cite id="erc-2021" /> A year later the Crimson reported that
    three of nine recommendations had not yet been carried out.<Cite id="crimson-2022-recs" />
  </p>

  <h3>Roland Fryer (Economics)</h3>
  <p>
    In July 2019 the Dean of the Faculty of Arts and Sciences announced that, following complaints of unwelcome sexual
    conduct in his research lab, Professor Fryer would be placed on administrative leave for two years, his lab would
    close, and conditions would apply on his return.<Cite id="gay-2019-fryer" /><Cite id="hm-2019-fryer" /> He denied
    engaging in discrimination or harassment. He returned to teaching and research in 2021 under
    conditions.<Cite id="crimson-2021-fryer" />
  </p>

  <h3>John Comaroff (Anthropology and African and African American Studies)</h3>
  <p>
    In January 2022 Harvard placed Professor Comaroff on unpaid leave and restricted his teaching and advising after
    internal reviews found that he had engaged in verbal conduct that violated its sexual harassment and professional
    conduct policies.<Cite id="harvard-2022-statement" /><Cite id="crimson-2022-comaroff" /> He has denied the
    allegations against him.
  </p>
  <p>
    In February 2022 three graduate students sued the University in federal court, alleging that it had ignored years
    of complaints. Professor Comaroff was not a defendant. The court largely declined to dismiss the case in 2023. It
    ended in August 2024 with a voluntary dismissal after mediation, on undisclosed terms, and without any ruling on the
    merits.<Cite id="crimson-2024-comaroff-suit" /> He retired in June 2024.<Cite id="crimson-2024-comaroff-retires" />
  </p>

  <h2 id="policy">Policy changes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>After the 2021 external review, Harvard’s president said the University accepted its recommendations and would centralise the review of personnel records.<Cite id="erc-2021" /></li>
    <li>Federal Title IX rules changed in 2020, and a 2024 revision was struck down in January 2025. Harvard has operated under interim policies adopted in 2020 and revised their definitions in February 2025.<Cite id="crimson-2025-titleix" /></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="epstein">The connection to the rest of this site</h2>
  <p>
    Harvard’s 2020 review of Epstein’s access said it “did not find evidence” that he engaged with undergraduate
    students during his campus visits or as a Visiting Fellow.<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /> That statement is
    limited to undergraduates and to what the review could establish. No source reviewed here documents an assault or
    harassment complaint at Harvard arising from his visits.
  </p>

  <h2 id="help">If you need support</h2>
  <p>
    This site is a research reference and cannot advise on individual situations. In the United States, the National
    Sexual Assault Hotline is available at 800-656-4673 and at rainn.org. Harvard students and staff can find the
    University’s current confidential resources on its own website.
  </p>
</Base>
